{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"After escalation, an unavailable second implementer can cause the factory to spend another paid round on an exhausted model instead of stopping."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An environment-blocked verification can be retried on the same model, wasting a paid attempt and potentially stopping the run without the intended reassessment."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"The warning misattributes the repeated verifier to the implementer, misleading the operator about the routing decision."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A single-entry review chain can leave a panel finding without an eligible verifier and stop the run for human intervention, although the reproduced refusal enforces the panel's independence rule."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A pinned decision model's usable last-resort answer is discarded, causing an unnecessary stop for human intervention."},{"id":"C6","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"The pinned group can select the implementer's vendor while another vendor is available, making the resulting availability message inaccurate; the selection itself follows the stated override of soft independence preferences."},{"id":"C3b","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"The warning attributes a finder-vendor match to the implementer, misexplaining the independence preference that was overridden."},{"id":"C6b","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"The completed-review message incorrectly says no other vendor was available when pinned routing selected the implementer's vendor despite an available alternative."}]}